{"schema":"plectis_maths_universe_excerpts_v2","paper":"erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface","excerpts":[["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:mersenne-channel-survival","Denominators of finite Mersenne sums","<p><em> Let <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>h</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> be integers, and let <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> be squarefree with every prime\nfactor at most <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi></math></span>. Let <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>P</mi></math></span> be a set of prime divisors of <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>r</mi></math></span> such that <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>p</mi></math></span> for each <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>P</mi></math></span>, and put <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></munder><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>. Then </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mfrac><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">gcd</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\" fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" symmetric=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mtext> </mtext><mi>den</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo></mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> In particular, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>C</mi></math></span> divides this reduced denominator when\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mo movablelimits=\"true\">gcd</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>. The factors in <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>C</mi></math></span> are pairwise coprime, since <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo movablelimits=\"true\">gcd</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>q</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">gcd</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> for\ndistinct primes. The assertion concerns this finite signed sum; it does\nnot replace the approximation hypotheses in Theorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#thm:full-support-catalogue\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:full-support-catalogue\">6.14</a>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:mersenne-channel-growth","Exponential growth of the reduced\ndenominators","<p><em> For an integer <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></math></span>, put </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>lcm</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></munder><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>den</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo></mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em> where the product\nis over primes. Then <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math></span>.\nIts exact denominator is </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">gcd</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:mobius-lambert-identity","The Lambert-series identity for the\nMöbius function","<p><em> The absolutely convergent signed series satisfies\n</em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em>\nThis classical identity is also recorded in <span class=\"citation\"\ndata-cites=\"duverneytachiya\"><a href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#ref-7\">[7]</a></span>. Expand each denominator\ngeometrically and interchange the absolutely convergent sums. The\ncoefficient of <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></math></span> is <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>, equal to <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn></math></span> for <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>0</mn></math></span> otherwise. This proves the identity.\nIts coefficients are <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, not\nindicators of a support; the identity therefore does not represent <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span> as a subseries with all coefficients\nin <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></math></span>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#cor:negative-mobius-overshoot","Negative-Möbius Boolean support\novershoots <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>","<p><em> Let <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>:</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></math></span>. Isolating the\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> term in Theorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#thm:mobius-lambert-identity\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:mobius-lambert-identity\">6.28</a> gives\n</em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mtable columnspacing=\"0em\" rowspacing=\"0.1em\"><mtr><mtd><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></munder><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>63</mn></mfrac><mo>&gt;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em> since <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>. This rules out the particular\ncandidate <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>N</mi></math></span>. It does not rule out\nother infinite supports representing <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:half-skip-dichotomy","The half-skip dichotomy via\nErdős–Borwein irrationality","<p><em> The half target satisfies </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝒜</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟺</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><msub><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∖</mo><mi>G</mi><mtext> is infinite</mtext><mo>,</mo></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em> where\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>G</mi></math></span> is the greedy support for <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>. In the forward direction, finitely\nmany skipped exponents would express the full Mersenne sum as <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span> plus a finite rational sum,\ncontradicting Erdős’s full-support irrationality theorem. The reverse\nimplication uses the greedy tail criterion: once a remainder exceeds the\nwhole available tail, every later exponent is selected. Infinitely many\nskips exclude that failure. The equivalence does not establish that\ninfinitely many skips actually occur.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:nine-way-hub","Equivalent descriptions of\nhalf-membership","<p><em> Each of the following is equivalent to <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝒜</mi></mrow></math></span>:</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li><p><em>The integer-greedy sequence is not eventually always on\nbranch <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></mrow></math></span>.</em></p></li>\n<li><p><em>There are arbitrarily large <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>s</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></math></span> with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>s</mi><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></math></span>.</em></p></li>\n<li><p><em>Upper or middle transitions occur at arbitrarily large\nrows.</em></p></li>\n<li><p><em>Some sequence <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo accent=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></math></span> satisfies <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></math></span> for every <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>j</mi></math></span>.</em></p></li>\n<li><p><em>There are rows <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo accent=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></math></span> and omitted ranks <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub></math></span> with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo accent=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></math></span>.</em></p></li>\n<li><p><em>The positive ranks omitted by the real half-greedy rule form\nan infinite set.</em></p></li>\n<li><p><em>The real half-greedy rule has no last omitted positive\nrank.</em></p></li>\n</ol>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#lem:eventually-right-impossible","The values on either side of an eventual\nright continuation","<p><em> If the seam eventually always extends “true”\n(right branch) from some row <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>S</mi></math></span> on\nwith a fixed lower prefix <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>u</mi></math></span>, the\nresulting cofinite-support value stays strictly below <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>; the matching alternative “upper\ncompetitor” word gives a strict excess <em>above</em> <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>. Neither of these two cofinite\ncontinuations represents <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>. This\ndoes not rule out an eventually-right integer orbit; it describes the\ntwo values in that case. The inequalities are used in Theorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#thm:final-middle-cell\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:final-middle-cell\">6.44</a>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#lem:mersenne-tail-weight","Each Mersenne weight exceeds its remaining\ntail","<p><em> For <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, let <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></math></span>. Then </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> For the upper bound, compare\neach tail term with <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></math></span>\nat index <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></math></span>, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, and sum the geometric series. The\nlast inequality follows by comparing <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>. This strict term-versus-tail\ninequality is the separation hypothesis used in the greedy\narguments.</em></p>\n<p><em>It is not available for the totient weights <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></math></span>: the terms at indices\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>4</mn></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>5</mn></math></span> already sum to <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>16</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>32</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></msup></math></span>, and the\nremaining tail is positive. A general strict-tail argument therefore\ncannot be transferred to that sequence without a different hypothesis or\nproof.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:greedy-survival-catalogue","Greedy survival and membership","<p><em>\nWith the greedy remainder <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>\nand complete tail <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></math></span>, </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝒜</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟺</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mi>x</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn><mtext> </mtext><mtext>and</mtext><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mtext> for every </mtext><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0.</mn></math></span></div>\n<p><em> If all\ninequalities hold, the nonnegative remainders tend to zero because <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo accent=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>, so the greedy partial sums\nconverge to <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>x</mi></math></span>. Conversely, strict\ntail domination <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></math></span> forces\nthe greedy choices in any representation and therefore gives every\ninequality. The argument applies to positive summable weights satisfying\nthat separation hypothesis; it is not a consequence of summability\nalone.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#lem:rank-step-trichotomy","The two next-prefix intervals and their\ngap","<p><em> Let <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span> be an integer,\nlet <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>u</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></math></span>, and\nsuppose the target <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi></math></span> lies in the\ninterval <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></math></span>. At\ndepth <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, either <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi></math></span> lies in the lower interval </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>,</mo></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em> or in the upper\ninterval </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>,</mo></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em>\nor in the open gap between them. These alternatives are disjoint because\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></math></span>, and exhaustive\nbecause <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></math></span>. For\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>, Lemma <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#lem:half-endpoint-kills\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#lem:half-endpoint-kills\">6.38</a> also\nexcludes the interval endpoints, so all relevant comparisons are strict.\nEndpoint exclusion is not what makes the two child intervals disjoint;\nthe strict-tail inequality does that.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#lem:fatal-gap-exclusion","A greedy gap excludes every\nrepresentation","<p><em> Let <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span> be\nan integer and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>u</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></math></span> a finite prefix.\nIf </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em>\nthen no support agreeing with <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>u</mi></math></span>\nthrough rank <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>d</mi></math></span> represents <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi></math></span>: omitting <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> leaves value at most the lower\nendpoint, while including it gives value at least the upper endpoint.\nOther length-<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>d</mi></math></span> prefixes have\ndisjoint containing intervals by the first-difference argument using\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></math></span>. Since the displayed gap\nlies inside the interval for <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>u</mi></math></span>,\nnone of those prefixes can represent <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi></math></span> either. Thus a certified strict gap\nexcludes every representation, not just one proposed\ncontinuation.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#lem:half-endpoint-kills","No finite Mersenne sum equals one half","<p><em> For every finite <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>u</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></math></span>, <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>:\nits reduced denominator is odd. Also <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span> for every <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>, since <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></math></span> is the irrational full Mersenne sum\nminus a finite rational sum. These observations exclude equality at the\nfinite-prefix and complete-tail endpoints of Lemma <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#lem:rank-step-trichotomy\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#lem:rank-step-trichotomy\">6.36</a>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#lem:straddle-agrees-greedy","Straddle words are canonical: they agree\nwith the greedy prefix","<p><em> Let <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>u</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></math></span> satisfy <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></math></span>. Then </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>∩</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo>,</mo></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em> where <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>G</mi></math></span> is the real greedy support for <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>. At the first disagreement, if <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>u</mi></math></span> takes rank <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>k</mi></math></span> and the greedy rule skips it, then\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>. In the opposite\ncase, the greedy remainder before <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>k</mi></math></span>\nis at least <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></math></span>, whereas the\nremaining contribution allowed by <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>u</mi></math></span>, including <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></math></span>, is at most <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></math></span>. Both cases contradict the\ndisplayed interval. Thus there is at most one such word at each depth;\nthe lemma does not assert its existence at every depth.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:last-skip-iff-fatal","Last-skip iff local fatality: a\npointwise criterion","<p><em> Keep <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>G</mi></math></span>\nfor the selected half-greedy support, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> for its remainder and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></math></span> for the complete tail after rank\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>M</mi></math></span>. A positive rank <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>M</mi></math></span> is the last omitted rank if and only if\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>M</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi>G</mi></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></math></span>. Consequently, </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝒜</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟺</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mtext>for every positive </mtext><mi>M</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Thus\nit suffices to check the tail inequality at the ranks actually omitted\nby the greedy rule. This is an equivalence on that fixed orbit; it does\nnot establish the inequality at its unboundedly many possible omitted\nranks.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#lem:seam-upper-or-middle","Seam upper-or-middle classification","<p><em>\nAt a row <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>s</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></math></span>, the terminal rank\nis omitted in the next row, <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>s</mi><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></math></span>, if and only if either </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>≤</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em> or this inequality\nfails and </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mn>4</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mrow><mi>rem</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>4.</mn></math></span></div>\n<p><em>\nThese are exactly branches <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">U</mi></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi></mrow></math></span> of\nTheorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#thm:dynamics\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:dynamics\">5.16</a>. On\neither branch the new terminal weight does not fit; on <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></mrow></math></span> it does. This is an exact\nclassification, not a conjecture inferred from observed branch\nlabels.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#lem:largest-false-rank-algebra","The exact gap at the largest omitted\nrank","<p><em> Let <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>s</mi></math></span> and\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>2</mn><mi>s</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>d</mi></math></span>. Write <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></munder><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">⌊</mo><msup><mn>4</mn><mi>s</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>e</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">⌋</mo></math></span> for a finite set <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>E</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></math></span>. For <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>u</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></math></span>, put <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>E</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>∪</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>E</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>∪</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>d</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></math></span>. Then </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>⋅</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>⋅</mo><msup><mn>4</mn><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em>\nThe correction is independent of the common prefix <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>u</mi></math></span>. For the integer-greedy rows <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>s</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></math></span>, a right transition preserves the\nlargest omitted rank, whereas an upper or middle transition makes <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>s</mi></math></span> the largest omitted rank in the next\nrow.</em></p>"]],"source":"erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.json?v=d132333b629d"}
